8. AI / Computer Vision Approach

The project uses MediaPipe Hands, a computer-vision framework that detects a hand and provides landmark coordinates for the hand.

The system does not need to train a separate deep-learning model from scratch. Instead, it uses a pre-trained hand landmark model.

The detected landmarks are used to calculate relationships between fingers and determine the current gesture.

For example:

If the index finger is extended, the system interprets it as cursor movement.
If the thumb and index finger come close together, it can be interpreted as a click.
If two fingers are raised, the system can perform scrolling.
If the thumb is raised, the system can control volume.
9. Main Features
🖱️ Mouse Movement

The user moves their index finger in front of the webcam.

The system converts the finger's position into screen coordinates and moves the mouse cursor accordingly.

👆 Click

The user brings the thumb and index finger together.

The system detects the distance between the two landmarks and performs a mouse click.

✌️ Scroll

The user raises two fingers.

The system interprets the gesture as a scrolling command.

👍 Volume Control

A thumbs-up gesture can increase the system volume.

A thumbs-down gesture can decrease the volume.

✊ Stop/Control

A closed fist can be used for another computer action, such as scrolling down or temporarily stopping control.

